# Expectation record for 06_control_flow/008_goto_and_labels.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 008_goto_and_labels
area               = 06_control_flow
description        = Forward and backward goto, a jump leaving two nested loops and an inner block at once, a jump into a deeper block, a label with a null statement, the single-exit cleanup idiom, and a goto leaving a switch
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour, and specifically free of the two
hazards that jumps introduce. First, no jump enters the scope of a
variably-modified type: this program declares no variable-length array at all,
so the hazard cannot arise. Second, no jump bypasses an initialisation whose
value is then read: every object is declared at the top of its function with no
initialiser and is assigned by an ordinary assignment statement, so a goto can
skip an assignment but can never skip a declaration's initialiser, and no object
is ever read while indeterminate. The two blocks that a goto enters declare no
objects at all, which is what makes those jumps well defined. Every label is the
target of at least one goto, so no label is unused. All arithmetic is on int
values of small magnitude, the largest being 511, so no signed overflow is
possible; the only remainder operation is i % 2 on a non-negative int. There are
no shifts, no pointers, no arrays and no type punning, therefore no aliasing
violation and no dependence on padding bytes. No object is modified twice
between sequence points. The three multi-argument printf calls pass only calls
to pure helper functions that read no mutable global, write no global and touch
no volatile object, so although their relative evaluation order is unspecified
it is unobservable and no dependence on unspecified order exists. Every volatile
object is read exactly once per use into a plain local or as a single argument.
The hand-written goto loop places its continue-equivalent label before the
increment, so the increment always executes and the loop always terminates;
every other loop has a small fixed bound.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Nothing implementation-defined is observed. Only int values are printed, so the
measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned
on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot affect the output, and the measured width
difference for long and pointers (4 bytes on i686, 8 elsewhere) is never exposed
because no long, pointer, size_t or ptrdiff_t value is printed and no sizeof
result appears in the output. No address is printed and no label address is
taken, so this program contains no computed goto; that construct belongs to the
GCC extensions area. No floating point is used. All four targets and all three
optimization levels are therefore compared, and all three oracles are enabled.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
forward_goto=1
backward_goto sum=15 i=5
goto_out_of_nested sum=4 steps=5
goto_into_block_taken=10
goto_into_block_not_taken=511
null_statement_label=0
goto_as_continue sum=6
cleanup_path=111 222 300
runtime_cleanup_path_first=111
runtime_cleanup_path_second=222
runtime_cleanup_path_none=300
switch_goto=1 12 3
runtime_switch_goto_one=1
runtime_switch_goto_two=12
runtime_switch_goto_default=3
goto_loop=0 1 55
runtime_goto_loop_zero=0
runtime_goto_loop_ten=55
END
